Reuse unchanged project descriptions without a background process

On Linux, plain $(D dub describe) can reuse a validated on-disk description
before initializing the D runtime. The cache records recipes, selections,
settings, dependency registrations, discovered directories, Git references,
compiler configuration, executable identities, and shared libraries. Changes
to the environment also invalidate it. The first invocation and invalidated
entries run normal discovery and additionally collect the cache inputs.

The cache-validation fast path runs before druntime initialization. Cache
building and input discovery then use Linux procfs, POSIX identity/file
syscalls, and supported ld-linux ELF loader information. Other platforms
continue to use the ordinary $(D dub describe) command.
Unsupported Linux runtime loaders or discovery inputs also fall back to the
ordinary command.

Root-prefix source and import globs such as $(D *.d), $(D **/*.d), or
$(D extra*/...) may traverse the package directory itself during discovery.
When that traversal reaches cache-managed state such as $(D .dub), caching is
disabled for that invocation and $(D dub describe) falls back to its ordinary
uncached behavior. These glob patterns retain their existing semantics.

The initial implementation supports native DMD and falls back to the ordinary
command for other compilers, compiler wrappers, generation hooks, file copying,
Git configuration includes, pkg-config resolution, and additional command-line arguments. Corrupted
or unwritable caches also use the ordinary command. Set
$(D DUB_NO_DESCRIBE_CACHE=1) to bypass the cache explicitly.
